1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"keep-the-uncertainty","number":"06","title":"Draw the uncertainty with the estimate.","mode":"Table search","scale":"slide","dek":"Display uncertainty alongside the estimates you discuss. A listener should be able to see both the estimated magnitude and how precisely it is measured.","before":[{"title":"Peer-response table · PDF p. 58","kind":"image","alt":"Four peer comparisons, two liquidity outcomes and two specifications; coefficients have standard errors below them.","image":"/originals/silva-p58.png","id":"peer-estimates","sourcePage":"58","provenance":"Original slide · Second liquidity-creation column; arrows run from responding banks to peers. Parentheses: bank-clustered standard errors."}],"after":[{"title":"Large banks’ response to small peers remains uncertain.","kind":"bankPeerCoefficients","axisLabel":"Response coefficient: liquidity creation","rowLabelHeader":"Responding banks / peers","domain":[-0.65,1.65],"note":"Bank and year fixed effects. Approx. 95% intervals: estimate ± 1.96 × bank-clustered SE.","alt":"Liquidity-creation peer responses with approximate 95% intervals: large/large 0.773 [0.422,1.124]; large/small 0.045 [−0.529,0.619]; small/small 0.803 [0.072,1.534]; small/large 0.886 [0.510,1.262].","label":"BANKS’ LIQUIDITY CREATION","revises":["peer-estimates"]}],"diagnosis":"Standard errors are present, but the audience must mentally turn them into uncertainty. Plot one complete comparison with intervals.","preserved":"All four coefficients and standard errors from the second liquidity-creation column, including the near-zero estimate.","deferred":"Other outcome and specification. No inference about differences between coefficients is added.","context":"Actual EBA research talk. The original uses blue type for significant rows; the repair adds interval width. The companion paper spells out the direction hidden by the slide’s arrows.","sourceType":"public","source":{"authors":"André Silva","title":"Strategic complementarity in banks’ funding liquidity choices and financial stability","date":"2015-11-19","url":"https://eba.europa.eu/documents/10180/1018121/Silva%2B-%2BStrategic%2Bcomplementarity%2Bin%2Bbanks%27%20funding%2Bliquidity%2Bchoices%2Band%2Bfinancial%2Bstability%2B-%2BPresentation.pdf/6c025e56-3737-45bd-a59d-bed72647afca","pages":"58 (printed 14/17)","event":"4th EBA Policy Research Workshop","eventURL":"https://www.eba.europa.eu/publications-and-media/events/4th-annual-research-workshop-financial-regulation-and-real-economy","companionPaperURL":"https://www.eba.europa.eu/sites/default/files/documents/10180/1018121/1a9009ba-aa10-496c-9b20-9c1bf1834261/Silva%2520-%2520Strategic%2520complementarity%2520in%2520banks%2527%2520funding%2520liquidity%2520choices%2520and%2520financial%2520stability%2520-%2520Paper.pdf","companionPages":"50 (Table 7), 20 (measurement)"},"point":"Interpreting an estimate requires information about its precision. Plotting estimates with clearly labeled confidence intervals lets the audience see their magnitudes and uncertainty together, with the relevant reference value marked. Include imprecise estimates when they belong to the comparison; they also matter for what the evidence establishes.","beforeCaption":"","afterCaption":"","principle":"Minimize tables with lots of numbers!","topic":"Banking","values":["Source column 2: liquidity creation with bank and year fixed effects. All four peer comparisons are retained.","Each interval is the reported coefficient ±1.96 times its bank-clustered standard error; these are approximate normal 95% intervals.","Responding banks / peers: large / large 0.773 (SE0.179), large / small 0.045 (0.293), small / small 0.803 (0.373), small / large 0.886 (0.192).","The original arrows run from responding banks to peers; the companion paper’s Table 7 spells out that direction. No test of differences between coefficients is shown."]}}]
